## 5 Fakes That Hide in Antique Shops

**The 5 fakes below are the five most common misrepresentations I correct when clients bring in "vintage" marquise rings from antique shops and estate sales.** Each one hides in plain sight if you don't know what to check. All five can be caught with the GIA Verification Protocol later in this guide.

### Fake 1 — Modern Marquise Sold as "Vintage"

The industry standard definition of "vintage" is 30 years or older. "Antique" is 100 years or older. Under 30 years = modern. Antique shops and online estate sellers frequently label rings dated 1995–2005 as "vintage" to charge a premium. The 2000s are not vintage. The 1990s mid-decade is a grey zone. Only pre-1995 pieces qualify as true vintage under the standard definition.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Industry Category</th><th>Example Years (as of {{year}})</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pre-1920</strong></td><td>Antique (100+ years)</td><td>1800s–1925</td><td>Highest value. Hand-cut stones. Rare.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>1920s–1930s</strong></td><td>Art Deco antique</td><td>1920–1939</td><td>Classic Art Deco period. Baguettes + geometric.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1940s–1960s</strong></td><td>Mid-century vintage</td><td>1940–1969</td><td>Transitional cuts. Modern marquise appears mid-1950s.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>1970s–1996</strong></td><td>Late vintage (30+ years old)</td><td>1970–1996</td><td>Qualifies as vintage. GIA grading becomes standard.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1997–present</strong></td><td>Modern (NOT vintage)</td><td>1997–{{year}}</td><td>Modern. Sellers who label this "vintage" are misrepresenting.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Check the ring's date of manufacture before accepting a "vintage" label. Legitimate dealers provide provenance documentation (purchase receipts, appraisals dated 20+ years ago, estate records). If the only "vintage" claim is the seller's word, assume modern until proven otherwise.

### Fake 2 — Re-Polished Stones with Removed GIA Inscriptions

Every GIA-graded diamond since the 1990s has a laser-etched report number on the girdle. The inscription is microscopic, visible only with a 10x+ loupe. It is also permanent. Shady re-cutters grind the inscription off the girdle during re-polishing to "orphan" the stone from its original cert, then sell the stone as unknown-provenance "vintage" without disclosing the modern origin.

The giveaway: a loose stone or ring that the seller cannot provide a GIA report for, combined with an unusually clean girdle under 10x loupe magnification. If the girdle has polish lines but no inscription, the inscription was ground off.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Stone Status</th><th>GIA Inscription Present?</th><th>Report Available?</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Properly certified GIA</strong></td><td>Yes (laser-etched)</td><td>Yes (seller provides)</td><td>Legitimate. Verify the report number online.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Pre-1990 antique</strong></td><td>No (GIA laser etching not standard yet)</td><td>No or paper-only</td><td>Expected for genuine antique. Appraisal needed.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Re-polished to remove inscription</strong></td><td>No (removed)</td><td>No ("lost" cert)</td><td>Fake vintage. Modern stone orphaned from its cert.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Lab-grown</strong></td><td>Yes (IGI inscription usually)</td><td>Yes (seller SHOULD provide)</td><td>Not vintage. Lab marquise didn't exist pre-2010.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Check for GIA laser inscription with a 10x loupe. If the stone has polish signs but no inscription, assume it was ground off. If the seller claims "the cert was lost in 1960," the stone is likely post-1990 modern that had its inscription removed.

### Fake 3 — Lab-Grown Stone Passed Off as Pre-1950 Natural

Commercial lab-grown diamond production for engagement-size stones did not exist until the mid-2010s. Marquise lab stones specifically became widely available around 2015–2018. Any seller claiming a "pre-1950 lab-grown vintage marquise" is lying. Lab-grown technology for gem-quality marquise did not exist before 2010 at any scale.

The giveaway: HPHT or CVD lab-grown stones have distinct inclusion patterns under magnification — different from natural stone inclusions. A professional appraisal catches this in minutes.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Stone Claim</th><th>Reality Check</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>"Pre-1950 natural marquise"</strong></td><td>Possible. Hand-cut, often warm colour (I-K)</td><td>Verify with appraisal + cut-era analysis.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>"1960s–1990s natural marquise"</strong></td><td>Possible. Modern-cut, GIA cert often available</td><td>Verify with GIA inscription check.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>"Pre-2010 lab-grown marquise"</strong></td><td>Not possible. Commercial lab marquise is post-2015.</td><td>Lie. Walk away.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>"Antique lab-grown"</strong></td><td>Oxymoron. Lab-grown IS modern.</td><td>Lie or confused seller.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>"Vintage lab marquise (post-2015)"</strong></td><td>Fine if under $600 lab price + modern setting</td><td>Legitimate "vintage-style" setting with lab stone.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Any "antique" or "pre-2010" lab-grown claim is false. Lab-grown marquise is a 2015-onward technology. Natural antique and vintage marquise exist and have provenance. Mixed up labels cover for pricing fraud — walk away.

### Fake 4 — Reproduction Setting with Modern Stone

The setting itself may be a genuine 1920s Art Deco mount with authentic milgrain beading and period scrollwork. But the centre stone was dropped in last year. Legally and ethically, a "vintage ring" requires both setting AND stone to be vintage. A genuine 1920s mount + modern 2020s GIA-cert marquise = "vintage-style ring with modern stone," not "vintage ring."

This fake exists because old settings outlast the stones they held. Antique dealers acquire empty vintage mounts, drop in modern stones, and sell the composite as "vintage." The setting is real. The ring is not.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Component Status</th><th>Setting</th><th>Centre Stone</th><th>Legitimate Label</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>True vintage ring</strong></td><td>Pre-1990</td><td>Pre-1990</td><td>Vintage ring</td><td>Both components vintage. Highest value.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Vintage setting + modern stone</strong></td><td>Pre-1990</td><td>Post-2010</td><td>Vintage-style ring with modern stone</td><td>Common mislabel. Setting old, stone new.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Modern reproduction setting + vintage stone</strong></td><td>Post-2010 (vintage-style)</td><td>Pre-1990</td><td>Vintage stone in reproduction setting</td><td>Collectible stone, modern mount.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Full modern reproduction</strong></td><td>Post-2010 (vintage-style)</td><td>Post-2010</td><td>Vintage-style modern ring</td><td>What major retailers actually sell. Honest + GIA-cert.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Ask the seller: when was the setting made? When was the stone cut? If both pre-1990, legitimate vintage. If setting is old and stone is new, it is a vintage-style ring with modern stone — fair price should reflect the mixed origin. Overpaying for the composite as "fully vintage" is the fake.

### Fake 5 — Moissanite or CZ in a Vintage Mount

Antique mounts fit any colourless stone of the right dimensions. Unscrupulous sellers mount a $200 moissanite or $20 cubic zirconia in a $5,000 period setting and sell the whole piece as a $10,000 vintage diamond ring. The visual difference between moissanite and diamond is subtle to the naked eye — most buyers cannot tell without instruments.

The giveaway: a thermal conductor tester (jeweller's "diamond tester") detects moissanite and CZ instantly. Any legitimate antique dealer uses one. Any seller who refuses to let you test the stone is hiding something.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Stone Type</th><th>Thermal Conductor Test</th><th>Electrical Conductor Test</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Natural diamond</strong></td><td>Pass</td><td>Pass (double-tester required)</td><td>Legitimate diamond.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Lab-grown diamond</strong></td><td>Pass</td><td>Pass</td><td>Legitimate diamond, modern origin.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Moissanite</strong></td><td>Pass (fools thermal-only tester)</td><td>Fails (double-tester catches it)</td><td>Common fake. Needs double-tester to detect.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Cubic zirconia (CZ)</strong></td><td>Fails</td><td>Fails</td><td>Easiest fake to catch.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>White sapphire</strong></td><td>Fails</td><td>Fails</td><td>Easy to catch with tester.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Any purchase from an antique shop requires a thermal + electrical conductor test. Insist on a professional appraisal with instruments before transferring money. A seller who refuses testing is selling a fake. Legitimate dealers welcome testing because their stones pass.

> **"The last five 'vintage' marquise rings I inspected for clients at antique shops were all fakes to some degree. Two had moissanite centres in genuine 1920s mounts. One was a 2015 GIA-cert stone sold as 'pre-WWII natural.' One had a ground-off GIA inscription and no cert. One was a full modern reproduction labelled as '1930s.' Zero were legitimate antique-stone + antique-setting pieces. The antique shop margin for a true-vintage marquise is small, so dealers pad with fakes. Buy from certified-market retailers for the vintage aesthetic without the fake risk."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## The Vintage vs Antique vs Reproduction Divide

**The Vintage vs Antique vs Reproduction Divide is the first thing to understand before shopping any "vintage marquise ring."** These three categories carry different values, different provenance needs, and different fake risk. Mixing them up is the single biggest source of overpayment in the vintage marquise market.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Age Range</th><th>Typical Price Range</th><th>Fake Risk</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Antique</strong></td><td>100+ years (pre-1925)</td><td>$5,000–$50,000+</td><td>High (hand-cut hard to verify)</td><td>Rare. Requires expert appraisal. Highest fraud risk.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Art Deco antique</strong></td><td>1920s–1939</td><td>$3,000–$30,000</td><td>Very High (most-faked period)</td><td>Classic baguette + geometric style. Heavy reproduction market.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vintage</strong></td><td>30–100 years (1925–1996)</td><td>$2,000–$15,000</td><td>High (middle period, most faked for premium)</td><td>Transitional cuts + early modern. GIA cert may exist.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Vintage-style modern retail</strong></td><td>Post-2010 (reproduction)</td><td>$1,100–$5,500</td><td>None (sold honestly)</td><td>What major retailers sell. GIA-cert stones + modern warranty.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Custom vintage-inspired</strong></td><td>Any (made to order)</td><td>$3,000–$15,000+</td><td>None</td><td>Bespoke modern piece in vintage style.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** If you want the vintage aesthetic without the fake risk, buy "vintage-style modern retail" from certified-market retailers. If you want a genuine antique or vintage piece, buy only from GIA-appraised dealers with documented provenance. Antique shops without provenance documentation are the highest-risk purchase channel.

[![Vintage marquise diamond ring in 14k white gold shown from above with vintage profile and classic silhouette against a white backdrop](/images/vintage-marquise-diamond-ring-white-gold-top-view.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=vintage&ShapeID=Marquise&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=Image)

## The GIA Verification Protocol

**The GIA Verification Protocol is the 4-step check that catches 90% of fake vintage marquise rings.** Each step takes 5–15 minutes. Any legitimate antique dealer will cooperate with all 4 steps. Any seller who refuses one or more is hiding something.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What to Check</th><th>Time Required</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Step 1 — GIA inscription loupe</strong></td><td>10x loupe scan of girdle for laser-etched report number</td><td>5 min</td><td>Catches ground-off inscriptions and lab-grown IGI stones.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Step 2 — GIA online report lookup</strong></td><td>Verify inscription number on GIA Report Check site</td><td>5 min</td><td>Confirms the report exists and matches the stone.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Step 3 — Thermal + electrical conductor test</strong></td><td>Double-tester for diamond vs moissanite vs CZ</td><td>2 min</td><td>Catches Fake 5 (moissanite/CZ in vintage mount).</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Step 4 — Professional appraisal + provenance documentation</strong></td><td>Independent appraiser inspects + reviews provenance paperwork</td><td>45 min (plus $75–$200 fee)</td><td>Catches Fakes 1, 2, 4. Required for pieces over $5,000.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Run all 4 steps on any vintage marquise purchase over $2,000. The $75–$200 appraisal fee is cheap insurance against a $5,000–$50,000 fake. If the seller refuses step 1, 2, or 3, walk away immediately. If the seller refuses step 4, the piece is almost certainly a fake.

## The Old Mine Cut Spotting Guide

**The Old Mine Cut Spotting Guide helps distinguish genuine pre-1900 marquise stones from modern reproductions.** Old mine cut marquise diamonds have specific visual signatures that modern machine-cut marquise cannot replicate — chunky facets, a visible culet (small flat facet at the stone's bottom), warm colour from imperfect lighting-era cutting, and often slight asymmetry from hand-cutting.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Old Mine Cut (pre-1900)</th><th>Modern Cut (post-1950)</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Culet visibility</strong></td><td>Visible flat facet at bottom</td><td>Pointed, invisible (modern cutting)</td><td>Antique stones have visible culets. Modern stones don't.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Facet count</strong></td><td>48–52 facets (hand-cut variance)</td><td>58 facets (standard brilliant)</td><td>Count visible facets under loupe.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Symmetry</strong></td><td>Slight asymmetry (hand-cut)</td><td>Perfect symmetry (machine-cut)</td><td>Hand-cut irregularity = antique indicator.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Colour grade typical</strong></td><td>I–K (warm from imperfect era lighting)</td><td>D–H typical (optimised cutting)</td><td>Antique stones often warm.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fluorescence</strong></td><td>Often strong-very strong</td><td>Variable (0–Strong)</td><td>Strong fluorescence common in antique stones.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Girdle</strong></td><td>Thick, bruted (not polished)</td><td>Thin, polished smooth</td><td>Rough girdle = antique sign.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** True antique marquise stones have distinctive hand-cutting signatures that a professional appraiser can identify in minutes. If a stone sold as "antique" has a modern 58-facet symmetric cut, D–H colour, and polished girdle, it is a modern stone being mislabeled.

## Diamond Pairing Math — 1ct to 2.5ct Marquise Centre

A vintage-style setting is only half the ring. Pairing math for the most common marquise centre carat tiers:

